  ==========================================================================
  4. WHY THIS MODULE IS ARCHIVED
  ==========================================================================
  The migration must preserve security and permission-evaluation outcomes.
  The permission evaluator, and the reflection-based scanning that supports
  it, are unchanged by directive. This test class is the rule-evaluation unit
  surface itself. Across its 23 methods it covers rule matching by permission,
  by target type and by object status, including a status expressed as an
  array inside a rule document; role-set matching in its all-of, any-of,
  wildcard, case-insensitive and empty-set forms; parent fallback resolution
  when no rule matches the object itself; and participant-type checks for an
  assignee, a reader, a member of one of several required groups, a principal
  in none of the required types, and the two degenerate participant lists.
  CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY: a difference between this report and its
  baseline twin is not a minor regression. It is a security-outcome change,
  and it is blocking rather than absorbable. Of every report in this archive,
  this is the one where the guarantee that access decisions did not shift is
  most directly confirmed or falsified.
  The change set's one security-adjacent source edit lives in a DIFFERENT
  module, the login service, where a directory context-factory reference
  becomes the identical class name held as a string, which the naming
  environment consumes identically. Nothing in this module's source changes,
  so these 23 test methods are unaffected by that edit.

